**Raynaud's** (said "ray-nose") is when the small blood vessels in your **fingers and toes** narrow for a short time, usually when you are **cold**, **anxious or stressed**. This reduces the blood supply, so your fingers or toes change colour and feel numb or painful.

It is **very common**, and is more common in **women**. Sometimes it can affect other parts of the body, such as your ears, nose, lips or nipples.

## Symptoms

During an attack, the affected areas may:

- change colour, often turning **white**, then **blue**, then **red** as the blood comes back. On darker skin, the changes can be harder to see, and the skin may look paler or darker than usual
- feel **cold** and **numb**
- feel **painful**, or tingle with **pins and needles**, as they warm up

An attack can last from a few minutes to a few hours.

## Primary and secondary Raynaud's

- **Primary Raynaud's** happens on its own, with no other cause. It is the most common type, often starts between the ages of 15 and 30, and is usually mild.
- **Secondary Raynaud's** is caused by another condition or something else, such as:
  - autoimmune conditions, such as **scleroderma**, **lupus**, **rheumatoid arthritis** and **Sjögren's syndrome**
  - some **medicines**, such as beta blockers
  - **smoking**
  - regularly using **vibrating tools** at work, such as drills or chainsaws. This is called **hand-arm vibration syndrome**

## When to see a GP

See a GP if:

- your symptoms are **severe** or affect your daily life
- Raynaud's affects only **one side** of your body
- it started after the age of **30**
- you also have **joint pain**, a **skin rash**, **muscle weakness** or other new symptoms
- your child has symptoms and is **under 12**

These can be signs of secondary Raynaud's, which may need tests and treatment.

## Getting diagnosed

A GP can usually diagnose Raynaud's from your symptoms. They may ask you to take a photo of your fingers during an attack. You may have **blood tests** to check for conditions that can cause secondary Raynaud's. A specialist may look at the tiny blood vessels at the base of your nails with a magnifying tool. This is called **nailfold capillaroscopy**.

Read about [treatment for Raynaud's](/conditions/heart-and-circulation/raynauds/treatment/).

{% callout type="danger" title="Get help urgently" %}
Call **111** or contact your GP straight away if you have **sores or ulcers** on your fingers or toes that are not healing, or if the skin turns **black**. Call **999** if a finger or toe suddenly becomes very painful, cold and pale and does not recover when warmed.
{% /callout %}
